WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.290
- You can see how light and
fluffy this material is.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.300
- [Narrator] Trash, sorted, shredded,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.310
and piled into heaps.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.580
But when United Airlines
looks at this garbage,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.580
it sees jet fuel, or more specifically,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.250
sustainable aviation fuel known as SAF,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.240
which many see as the key
to decarbonizing aviation.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.040
But there's a supply problem.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.160
Producers made about 15.8
million gallons of SAF

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.690
last year in the US,

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.100
major US airlines,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.060
used about 17.5 billion
gallons of jet fuel.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.800
- We don't have the solution

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.830
commercially available at scale

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.540
to really make a difference right now,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.430
but we do know what that solution is,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.380
and it's sustainable aviation fuel.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.150
- [Narrator] So airlines
and the government

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.000
are now pumping money into
the low-carbon fuel sector,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.060
investing millions in projects
like SAF produce from trash

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.060
to get this nascent
industry off the ground.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.660
But will it take off?
(jet roaring)

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.310
The aviation industry alone

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:03.150
accounted for more than 2%
of global emissions in 2021,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.000
and its share of the
pie is expected to grow.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.260
Unlike the auto industry,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.500
which is moving away from
fossil fuels to battery power,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.220
commercial jets still
need liquid fuel to fly.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.130
- If you look at our
greenhouse gas footprint

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.500
as an airline, a commercial airline,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.800
98% of the emissions that we create

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.300
and put into the atmosphere,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.830
are from the use of fuel.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.350
- [Narrator] SAF is what's
called a drop-in solution

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.770
because it can drop in in
place of petroleum fuel.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.700
It doesn't require new
infrastructure or even new planes.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.200
The SAF on the market today,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.540
can cut emissions by up to 80%

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.240
compared to conventional jet fuel.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.400
In Sparks, Nevada, Fulcrum BioEnergy

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.950
is working on a fuel
with a bigger promise.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.290
- We produce a fuel product that has

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.260
zero net impact on carbon emissions.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.700
- [Narrator] It starts with trash,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.960
about 350,000 tons of it each year.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.240
After sorting out things
like rocks and metals,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.200
the leftover garbage

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.780
is made into that confetti-like material.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.550
- There's paper, there's wrappings,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.110
there's plastic shredding.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.300
- [Narrator] It's the
feedstock for Fulcrum's fuel.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.910
- All of this material, what you're seeing

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.830
in this warehouse here,

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.130
will convert into about a
hundred thousand gallons

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.840
of transportation fuel.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.110
- [Narrator] But getting from
this to fuel isn't simple.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.140
- Creating that feedstock is
the first part of the process.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.990
The next stage of the
process is what you see here,

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.490
taking that feedstock

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:32.160
with the white conveyors
going up to the top

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.710
of this first gasification tower,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.020
beginning the process of transforming

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.390
that feedstock into usable gases.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.180
It's a three-stage gasification process.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.240
What you're looking at
here is the first stage.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.190
- [Narrator] The process
creates carbon monoxide

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.770
and hydrogen molecules, known as syngas,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:50.820
which is then cleaned

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.010
and sent through this
Fischer-Tropsch System.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.740
- But here, those gases
are actually converted

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.650
into a liquid transportation fuel

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.500
that can be sold to refineries

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.440
or ultimately, will be,
you know, modifying,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.600
and we're upgrading into jet fuel.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.010
- [Narrator] Fulcrum opened this facility,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.380
its first commercial-scale plant last year

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.720
and it says it will
produce 11 million gallons

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.260
of its synthetic crude oil
product here by next year.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.350
United, Cathay Pacific,
Japan Airlines, and others,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.720
have already signed offtake
agreements with Fulcrum

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.553
that can see the company

00:03:22.553 --> 00:03:26.310
delivered 290 million
gallons of its fuel annually.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.290
- It really is all about supply.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.120
I mean, let's call it what it is.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.720
There's not a lack of
demand for low-carbon SAF.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.240
- [Narrator] Which is why
outside of Fulcrum's Nevada plant

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.190
25 SAF production refineries

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.893
are now planned or open in the US alone.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.770
United is already pumping
a blend of jet fuel and SAF

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.290
onto its flights here, out of Los Angeles,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.860
as well as San Francisco,
and Amsterdam Airports.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.100
In 2022, it used 3 million gallons of SAF.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.190
This year it expects,
that to hit 10 million,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.330
but United has a goal of
net zero emissions by 2050.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.540
- Overall, I need 4
billion gallons of fuel.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.240
So if we're talking about
10 million gallons of SAF,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.690
it is far less than 0.1%
of my total fuel supply,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.780
which is really where we get
to the crux of the issue.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.520
How do we generate more production?

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.290
How do we create more supply

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.170
so that it's not less
than 0.1% of my fuel?

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.100
- [Narrator] Earlier this year,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.860
the airline launched a
more than $100 million fund

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:26.940
to invest in startups

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.460
looking for new ways to
produce SAF from feedstocks,

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.450
including alcohol, forest
waste, and of course, trash.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.220
It even enlisted Oscar the Grouch

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.870
to draw attention to the effort.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.710
- Trash like this could be used for good?

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.860
- If SAF is gonna make
its way into the market

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.260
on a more broad-based,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.480
a more commonly used approach,

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.820
it's gonna have to be
new feedstock sources

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.983
that unleash larger volumes.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.590
- [Narrator] The SAF being pumped today,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:59.430
relies on used cooking oil and
animal fats as its feedstock.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.320
- Vegetable oils, fats,
waste oils, greases,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.600
are also used for fuels

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.210
for the on-road transportation sector.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.800
So the same feedstocks

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.710
are used for biodiesel
and renewable diesel.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.660
So there's competition.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.240
- [Narrator] About 6 million
metric tons of used cooking oil

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.820
and 27 million metric tons of animal fats

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.770
are traded globally every year,

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:27.120
which would cover only around
5% of jet fuel consumption.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.190
- There is not enough used cooking oil

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.100
in the world to fuel
aviation, that is true.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.270
But when you start looking at

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.610
the second-generation around trash,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.420
around residue from the
forest, from ethanol,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.290
we have 17 billion gallons
of ethanol in the US alone.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.180
There is plenty of feedstock there.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.940
It's a question of how
do you get the feedstock

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.590
to the right production location

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.070
so that you can actually create
sustainable aviation fuels

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.660
with a low carbon footprint?

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.130
- [Narrator] At least 30 airlines,

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.840
have promised to use SAF,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.290
usually for 10% of their
fuel consumption by 2030,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.420
and the Biden Administration
has set a goal

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.130
of scaling US SAF production

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.520
to 3 billion gallons per year by then too.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.630
SAP global projections

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.850
show the country won't quite hit that,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.730
but it will scale to more
than 2 billion gallons a year

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:15.930
by the target date.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.630
- We certainly see the framework,

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:23.160
and we'd certainly see the
corporate community taking steps

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.310
in order to increase
domestic SAF production.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:29.163
And so even if mandates are not being met,

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.550
progress is certainly being made.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.860
- [Narrator] Then,
there's the issue of cost.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.800
SAF is typically two to
four times more expensive

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.390
than regular fuel.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:42.180
- Airlines have tight margins,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.840
and customers are always
looking for the best price,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.010
and it's uncertain how much
tolerance consumers have

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.190
for increased prices by using biofuels.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.450
- [Narrator] The Inflation Reduction Act

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.550
set aside about $300 million

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.590
for low-emissions aviation technologies,

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:01.470
plus $500 million for biofuels.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:04.410
It also established two
separate tax credits for SAF

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.060
to help bring its cost per gallon

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.310
closer to conventional fuel.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:12.450
- The IRA was a
game-changer, a game-changer.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.000
It actually puts sustainable
aviation fuels on the map.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.270
This was really a signal to
the market, to producers,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.700
that the government is
committed to building a market

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.470
for sustainable aviation fuels,

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.060
and it is backing this

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.030
as the pathway to decarbonize aviation.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.080
- [Narrator] And while experts say

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.000
more incentives are also needed,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.630
the industry is now flying
with some wind at its back.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.190
- SAF is having a moment,

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.913
and that moment is gonna
continue for a very long time.

